.TH WIILANDD 1 "August 2026" "wiiland" "WiiLand Manual"

.SH NAME
wiilandd \- bridge Wii Remote input to shared Linux virtual evdev devices
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wiilandd
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B wiilandd --device
.RI number | /sys/path
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wiilandd
is the display-server-neutral Wii Remote input daemon. It reads hid-wiimote
events through
.B libxwiimote
and creates display-neutral virtual devices with Linux
.BR uinput .
Those devices appear as evdev nodes and are consumed by a Wayland compositor
through libinput, by the X.org input stack, and by SDL, Steam, Wine/Proton, and
native applications through their normal Linux input paths.
.PP
The ingestion and virtual-device path is the same in Wayland and X.org sessions;
the daemon does not connect to either display server. On X.org, the installed
InputClass policy suppresses matching raw Wii Remote input nodes to avoid
duplicate input, while leaving libxwiimote ingestion and
.B WiiLand Virtual *
devices unaffected.
.SH PAIRING
Pair a Wii Remote through the desktop Bluetooth UI or the current
.B bluetoothctl
workflow. Make the remote discoverable, then use:
.PP
.RS
.B bluetoothctl
.br
.B power on
.br
.B agent on
.br
.B default-agent
.br
.B scan on
.br
.B pair XX:XX:XX:XX:XX:XX
.br
.B trust XX:XX:XX:XX:XX:XX
.br
.B connect XX:XX:XX:XX:XX:XX
.br
.B scan off
.br
.B quit
.RE
.PP
Replace the address with the one reported by
.B scan on
; desktop Bluetooth panels provide the equivalent discovery, pairing, and
connection operations. The obsolete
.B xwiibind
pairing workflow is not part of WiiLand.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -h , " --help"
Show help and exit without loading configuration.
.TP
.BR -l , " --list"
List connected Wii Remote devices and exit. Combine with
.B --verbose
to include devtype and extension attributes from sysfs. This does not create
uinput devices or load configuration.
.TP
.BR --version
Print the wiilandd version and exit without loading configuration.
.TP
.BR -d , " --device " number | /sys/path
Bridge one device instead of monitoring all connected and hotplugged devices.
A number refers to the output of
.BR --list .
.TP
.BR -p , " --profile " gamepad | desktop | both
Select the virtual input profile. The default
.B gamepad
profile creates a virtual controller for games. The
.B desktop
profile creates a virtual mouse/keyboard device: D-pad repeat and valid IR
events move the pointer, A/B click, +/- send Enter/Escape, Home sends the
compositor overview key, and 1/2 send Page Down/Page Up.
.TP
.BR --backend " " uinput
Select the input backend. Only
.B uinput
is currently supported; it is the shared Linux virtual-input path for Wayland,
X.org, and non-display consumers.
.TP
.BR --pointer-speed " " 1-127
Set the desktop profile pointer step in pixels per repeat tick. The default is
16.
.TP
.BR --ir-speed " " 1-127
Set the desktop profile IR pointer gain. The default is 8, matching one eighth
of the raw IR camera delta.
.TP
.BR --ir-deadzone " " 0-127
Suppress desktop profile IR pointer deltas smaller than this value after gain
scaling. The default is 0.
.TP
.BR --ir-smoothing " " 0-95
Smooth desktop profile IR pointer coordinates by retaining this percentage of
the previous filtered position. The default is 0.
.TP
.BR --ir-tracking " " dual | centroid | first
Select IR blob tracking. The default
.B dual
uses the midpoint of the widest valid IR-dot pair, avoiding source-order swaps
when both sensor-bar LEDs are visible.
.B centroid
averages every valid dot.
.B first
preserves the legacy first-valid-source tracker.
.TP
.BR --ir-aim-mapping " " relative | absolute
Select IR aim mapping. The default
.B relative
emits camera-like motion deltas and requires no screen calibration.
.B absolute
maps the selected IR point through
.B ir-screen-*
calibration bounds for users who want Wii-menu-style positional aim.
.TP
.BR -c , " --config " path
Load a key=value configuration file. Command-line options override loaded
configuration.
.TP
.BR --no-config
Use built-in defaults instead of loading the default configuration hierarchy.
It cannot be combined with
.BR --config .
.TP
.BR -n , " --dry-run"
Enumerate and open devices without creating /dev/uinput devices or emitting
virtual input. Useful for smoke tests and service diagnostics.
.TP
.BR --check-config
Load configuration, apply command-line overrides, validate the resulting state,
and exit without enumerating devices. Useful for packaging, service preflight,
and configuration saves.
.TP
.BR --dump-config
Print the fully resolved configuration after loading files and applying
command-line overrides, then exit. This is the effective configuration consumed
by a normal daemon start.
.TP
.BR --axis-map
Print the virtual gamepad axis and button mapping used by the gamepad profile,
then exit. This is a hardware-validation aid, does not enumerate devices, and
does not load configuration.
.TP
.BR --validation-checklist
Print the required real-hardware validation matrix, then exit. This is a
hardware-report aid, does not enumerate devices, and does not load
configuration.
.TP
.BR --doctor
Print runtime readiness diagnostics and exit without enumerating Wii devices.
Configuration is loaded like a normal daemon start unless
.B --no-config
is given. The output classifies the active session as
.BR wayland ,
.BR x11 ,
.BR headless ,
or
.B unknown
from
.BR WAYLAND_DISPLAY ,
.BR DISPLAY ,
and
.BR XDG_SESSION_TYPE .
A Wayland session remains
.B wayland
when
.B DISPLAY
is also set; the separate
.B session.xwayland.available
field reports whether a local XWayland display is available.
The output also reports the raw session fields
.BR XDG_SESSION_TYPE
and
.BR XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP ,
the Wayland display and
.BR XDG_RUNTIME_DIR ,
resolved Wayland and X11 socket details, configured system and resolved user
configuration paths, and
.BR /dev/uinput
existence/readability/writability. These are informational environment and
filesystem checks; the daemon does not connect to either display server.
.TP
.BR --self-test
Run deterministic profile, config, button-map, IR, and pointer-motion tests, then exit.
This mode does not enumerate devices or open
.BR /dev/uinput .
.TP
.BR --trace-events [ =all | keys | axes | ir | motion-plus ]
Print sequenced, monotonic-timestamped decoded libxwiimote event records before
forwarding them. Optional filters reduce output to key events, absolute-axis
events, IR events, or MotionPlus events. IR events are reported as
.B ir
with source coordinates; MotionPlus events are reported as
.B motion-plus
with X/Y/Z axis payloads. Intended for hardware validation reports; combine with
.B --dry-run
to inspect devices without creating virtual input.
.TP
.BR --calibrate-aim
Capture accelerometer zero and MotionPlus gyro bias from a flat, still Wii
Remote and print persistent
.B wiilandd.conf
key=value lines. Place the Wii Remote face down with buttons against a stable
surface for the sample window. If only one sensor source produces a stable
window, the command prints that source's offsets and a warning for the other;
at least one stable source is sufficient for success.
.TP
.BR --aim-calibration-duration " " 1-30
Set the
.B --calibrate-aim
sample window in seconds. The default is 8.
.TP
.BR -v , " --verbose"
Print device lifecycle details.
.PP
At most one primary action such as
.BR --doctor ,
.BR --dump-config ,
.BR --check-config ,
.BR --self-test ,
or
.B --calibrate-aim
may be selected. With no primary action, the daemon runs normally. All
arguments are parsed before an action runs, so invalid trailing arguments and
conflicting actions are rejected regardless of order.
.SH PERMISSIONS
The daemon must be allowed to read the hid-wiimote input event nodes and write
to
.BR /dev/uinput .
The installed udev rules grant active-seat access with
.B TAG+="uaccess"
and retain an
.B input
group,
.B 0660
fallback. A system or user service still runs with the permissions of its
account; the daemon does not elevate privileges.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Unless
.B --no-config
is set,
.B wiilandd
loads the optional system file from the configured
.B sysconfdir/wiiland/wiilandd.conf
path (normally
.BR /etc/wiiland/wiilandd.conf )
first, then the optional user file at an absolute
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/wiiland/wiilandd.conf
or
.B $HOME/.config/wiiland/wiilandd.conf
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset or relative. A relative
.B HOME
does not produce an implicit user path. Later files override earlier values.
Use
.B --config
to load one required explicit file instead of this hierarchy; command-line
options override values loaded from it.
.B --config
and
.B --no-config
are mutually exclusive. Help, version, list, axis-map, and
validation-checklist modes do not load configuration, while diagnostics such
as
.B --doctor
do. The selected system and user paths are reported by
.BR --doctor .
Supported keys are:
.TP
.B backend=uinput
The shared Linux uinput backend. It is the only currently supported backend and
does not depend on a display server.
.TP
.B profile=gamepad|desktop|both
Default virtual input profile.
.TP
.B pointer-speed=1-127
Desktop profile pointer step.
.TP
.B ir-speed=1-127
Desktop profile IR pointer gain.
.TP
.B ir-deadzone=0-127
Desktop profile IR jitter deadzone after gain scaling.
.TP
.B ir-smoothing=0-95
Desktop profile IR smoothing percentage.
.TP
.B ir-tracking=dual|centroid|first
Choose the IR tracker used by both desktop IR pointer motion and IR motion aim.
The default
.B dual
tracks the midpoint of the widest valid source pair, so source index swaps do
not cause jumps when the sensor bar is visible.
.TP
.B ir-aim-mapping=relative|absolute
Choose whether IR aim emits relative deltas or calibrated absolute screen
position. The default
.B relative
needs no display setup and remains best for camera aim.
.TP
.B ir-screen-left|right|top|bottom=0-32767
Optional screen bounds used only when
.B ir-aim-mapping=absolute .
Right must exceed left and bottom must exceed top; invalid rectangles are
rejected during configuration validation. These values let users dial in TV
size, aspect ratio, overscan, and sensor-bar placement without making absolute
pointing mandatory.
.TP
.B aim-mode=off|mouse|right-stick
Enable modern motion aiming. The default
.B off
keeps the login/session-safe gamepad unchanged.
.B mouse
converts the selected sensor to relative
.BR REL_X / REL_Y
pointer aim.
.B right-stick
converts it to
.BR ABS_RX / ABS_RY
camera aim for Steam, Proton/Wine, SDL, and native games that read a standard
right stick.
.TP
.B aim-source=auto|ir|motion-plus|accelerometer
Select the motion-aim sensor. The default
.B auto
prefers valid IR camera deltas, then MotionPlus gyro axes, then accelerometer
tilt. Calibration offsets are applied when configured. Without
.B aim-accel-zero-*
keys, accelerometer aim keeps the compatibility fallback of taking the first
accelerometer event as neutral.
.TP
.B aim-activation=always|b|z|c
Choose whether motion aim is always active or gated by holding B, Nunchuk Z, or
Nunchuk C. The default is
.BR b .
.TP
.B aim-sensitivity=1-127
Motion aim output gain. The default is 16.
.TP
.B aim-deadzone=0-32767
Motion aim deadzone before gain. The default is 4.
.TP
.B aim-smoothing=0-95
Motion aim smoothing percentage. The default is 25.
.TP
.B aim-invert-x=yes|no
Invert motion aim X output. The default is no.
.TP
.B aim-invert-y=yes|no
Invert motion aim Y output. The default is no.
.TP
.B aim-accel-zero-x|y|z=-32768-32767
Persist the flat-surface accelerometer neutral point generated by
.BR --calibrate-aim .
All three accelerometer axes must be configured together; partial triples are
rejected. A complete accelerometer triple may be used without MotionPlus
calibration. When configured, accelerometer aim subtracts this point instead
of using first-event neutral fallback.
.TP
.B aim-motion-plus-bias-x|y|z=-32768-32767
Persist the still MotionPlus gyro bias generated by
.BR --calibrate-aim .
All three MotionPlus axes must be configured together; partial triples are
rejected. A complete MotionPlus triple may be used without accelerometer
calibration. When configured, MotionPlus aim subtracts this bias before
sensitivity, deadzone, and smoothing are applied.
.TP
.B aim-calibration-duration=1-30
Default calibration sample window used by
.BR --calibrate-aim .
The default is 8.
.TP
.B desktop.a|b|plus|minus|home|one|two=ACTION
Override desktop button output. Actions are
.BR left-click ,
.BR right-click ,
.BR enter ,
.BR escape ,
.BR overview ,
.BR page-up ,
.BR page-down ,
and
.BR disabled .
.TP
.B device.<syspath-substring>.profile=gamepad|desktop|both
Override the profile for devices whose sysfs path contains the substring.
.TP
.B device-type.<devtype-substring>.profile=gamepad|desktop|both
Override the profile for devices whose hid-wiimote devtype contains the
substring, such as
.BR gen10 ,
.BR gen20 ,
.BR balanceboard ,
or
.BR procontroller .
Rules are evaluated in file order; later matching rules win.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/wiiland/wiilandd.conf
Usual system-wide daemon defaults path. The installed location follows the
configured
.BR sysconfdir ;
.B --doctor
prints the exact compiled path.
.TP
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/wiiland/wiilandd.conf
Optional per-user overrides; when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset, the equivalent path is
.B $HOME/.config/wiiland/wiilandd.conf .
.TP
.B res/wiilandd.conf
Documented sample configuration installed as the system configuration and
available under the package documentation examples directory.
.TP
.B res/70-wiiland-uinput.rules
udev permissions for hid-wiimote event nodes and
.BR /dev/uinput .
The
.B uaccess
tag and
.B input
group fallback support active-seat users in both Wayland and X.org sessions.
.TP
.B res/70-udev-wiiland.rules
udev rule for writable hid-wiimote LED sysfs attributes used by tools such as
.BR xwiishow .
.TP
.B res/50-xorg-fix-wiiland.conf
Optional X.org InputClass policy for raw Wii Remote nodes; it does not filter
libxwiimote or WiiLand virtual devices.
.TP
.B res/wiilandd.service
Installed systemd user service with
.B WantedBy=default.target
.
Its
.B ExecCondition
runs
.B wiilandd --check-config
before
.B ExecStart
; invalid configuration therefore prevents startup. The unit uses
.B Restart=on-failure
with a 2-second delay. It also enables
.BR NoNewPrivileges ,
locks the process personality, denies writable executable memory and real-time
scheduling, restricts SUID/SGID changes and syscall architectures, and sets
.BR UMask=0077 .
Enable and start it with
.BR "systemctl --user enable wiilandd.service"
and
.BR "systemctl --user start wiilandd.service" .
.SH X.ORG INTEGRATION
When installed, the X.org configuration contains an
.B InputClass
named
.B Nintendo Wii Remote Raw Input Blacklist
that matches
.B Nintendo Wii Remote
on
.B /dev/input/event*
and sets
.B Option "Ignore" "on"
.
The Classic Controller and Pro Controller InputClass entries explicitly set
.B Ignore
to
.B off
for their matching products. This suppresses duplicate raw-node handling by
X.org only; it does not block hid-wiimote, libxwiimote, the daemon, or devices
named
.B WiiLand Virtual Controller
and
.B WiiLand Virtual Desktop
.
.PP
The same uinput devices therefore remain available to the X.org stack and to
Wayland compositors, SDL, Steam, Wine/Proton, and native applications.
.SH GAMEPAD MAPPING
The gamepad profile exposes core and extension buttons as evdev button codes and
extension sensors on stable evdev absolute axes so SDL, Wine/Proton, and native
input testers can validate each source separately.
.TP
.B Wii Remote
Core buttons use D-pad, South/East, Start/Select, Mode, and 1/2 button events.
Accelerometer X/Y/Z use
.BR ABS_THROTTLE ,
.BR ABS_RUDDER ,
and
.BR ABS_WHEEL .
.TP
.B Nunchuk
Stick X/Y use
.BR ABS_X / ABS_Y ;
accelerometer X/Y/Z use
.BR ABS_HAT1X ,
.BR ABS_HAT1Y ,
and
.BR ABS_HAT2X .
.TP
.B MotionPlus
X/Y/Z use
.BR ABS_GAS ,
.BR ABS_BRAKE ,
and
.BR ABS_HAT0X .
.TP
.B Motion aiming
When
.B aim-mode=right-stick
is enabled, IR, MotionPlus, or accelerometer aim is emitted on
.BR ABS_RX / ABS_RY .
When
.B aim-mode=mouse
is enabled, the same aim signal is emitted on
.BR REL_X / REL_Y
through a virtual desktop pointer device. This deliberately exposes generic
Linux input instead of spoofing a DualSense, DualShock, or Steam Controller.
.SS Motion aim calibration
Run
.BR "wiilandd --device 1 --calibrate-aim"
and keep the Wii Remote face down, buttons against a flat stable surface, for
the printed sample window. Short or unstable samples are rejected. If only
accelerometer or only MotionPlus samples are stable, the command still succeeds
and prints the available source's offsets with a warning for the unavailable or
unstable source. Paste the generated
.BR aim-accel-zero-* ,
.BR aim-motion-plus-bias-* ,
and
.B aim-calibration-duration
lines into the same config file that sets
.BR aim-mode .
The Qt control center exposes the same fields and a capture button in the
Validation tab.
.SS IR tracking and screen calibration
The default IR path is calibration-free relative tracking. It uses the
.B dual
tracker to pick the midpoint of the widest valid IR-source pair, which is stable
when the sensor bar reports its two LEDs in different source slots between
frames. Users can switch to
.B centroid
for multi-source averaging or
.B first
for legacy behavior.
.PP
Screen/corner calibration is optional. Set
.B ir-aim-mapping=absolute
and provide
.BR ir-screen-left ,
.BR ir-screen-right ,
.BR ir-screen-top ,
and
.B ir-screen-bottom
when a game or setup benefits from Wii-menu-style positional aim. Relative
desktop pointer motion and relative IR aim continue to work without these
bounds, so diverse screens and top/bottom sensor-bar placements do not become a
setup requirement.
.PP
.SS Gamepad axis normalization
Mainline hid-wiimote devices expose controller families with different raw
ranges. WiiLand normalizes signed sticks, accelerometers, MotionPlus, and the
whammy bar to -32768 through 32767. Triggers, the guitar touch strip, and drum
pressures on trigger-style axes use 0 through 1023; drum pressures sharing
signed stick axes use 0 through 32767. Balance Board sensors retain their 0
through 65535 values. This gives SDL, Steam Input, Wine/Proton, and native
games full axis travel instead of the small fraction produced by forwarding
raw extension values into wider virtual ranges.
.PP
.TP
.B Classic Controller
Left stick uses
.BR ABS_X / ABS_Y ;
right stick uses
.BR ABS_RX / ABS_RY ;
analog triggers use
.BR ABS_Z / ABS_RZ .
.TP
.B Wii U Pro Controller
Left stick uses
.BR ABS_X / ABS_Y ;
right stick uses
.BR ABS_RX / ABS_RY .
ZL/ZR are reported as
.BR BTN_TL2 / BTN_TR2
button events.
.TP
.B Guitar
Stick X/Y use
.BR ABS_X / ABS_Y ;
whammy and fret board use the valid generic uinput axes
.BR ABS_HAT3X
and
.BR ABS_HAT3Y ;
strum/fret buttons use dedicated guitar button events, and +/- use
.BR BTN_START / BTN_SELECT .
.TP
.B Drums
Pad X/Y use
.BR ABS_X / ABS_Y ;
left/right cymbals use
.BR ABS_RX / ABS_RY ;
left/right/far-right toms use
.BR ABS_Z ,
.BR ABS_RZ ,
and
.BR ABS_HAT3X ;
bass and hi-hat pedals use
.BR ABS_HAT3Y
and
.BR ABS_MISC ;
+/- use
.BR BTN_START / BTN_SELECT .
.TP
.B Balance Board
The four weight sensors use
.BR ABS_PRESSURE ,
.BR ABS_DISTANCE ,
.BR ABS_TILT_X ,
and
.BR ABS_TILT_Y .
.SH BACKEND
The default and only supported backend is Linux uinput. It is display-server
independent and is consumed through evdev/libinput by Wayland compositors and
the X.org input stack, as well as directly by SDL, Steam, Wine/Proton, and
native applications. WiiLand opens only the hid-wiimote interfaces needed by
the selected profile and aim source. An optional extension-open failure does
not discard an otherwise usable remote.
.PP
Event dispatch drains at most 256 events per pass. The monitor path reconciles
the device set at most once per second, and removes failed devices so they can
be observed and added again on later hotplug/reconciliation events. These
bounds keep pointer repeat responsive under sensor traffic and keep each
recovery pass bounded.
.PP
The gamepad profile is safe as a login default; use
.B --profile=desktop
or
.B --profile=both
when the Wii Remote should also drive the desktop pointer and keyboard.
Virtual devices are named
.B WiiLand Virtual Controller
and
.B WiiLand Virtual Desktop
.
.SH HARDWARE REPORT
The installed
.B wiilandd-hardware-report
command collects finite host, permission, configuration, doctor, axis-map,
device, and manual-validation diagnostics. Run it without arguments for the
host report, or pass a device number or sysfs path (as accepted by
.BR --device )
to continue into a live dry-run trace. The report may pass one trace selector,
such as
.BR --trace-events=ir
or
.BR --trace-events=motion-plus .
It reports whether required probes succeed; it does not claim that physical
hardware passed until the operator exercises the listed validation matrix.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
Older releases exposed the virtual identities
.B WiiLand Wayland Controller
and
.B WiiLand Wayland Desktop
.
The current identities are
.B WiiLand Virtual Controller
and
.B WiiLand Virtual Desktop
for every display server. Applications or stored controller profiles that
match device names may need to be remapped to the new names.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wiiland (7),
.BR libxwiimote (7),
.BR xwiishow (1)
